In the context of Marchenko-Pastur (MP) theory, studying large low-rank perturbations helps us understand how eigenvalues of a random matrix deviate from the bulk distribution and form spikes. When we model a matrix \( W \) as a sum of a random matrix \( W_{\text{rand}} \) and a large rank-1 perturbation \( \Delta_{\text{large}} \), such that:

\[ W = W_{\text{rand}} + \Delta_{\text{large}}, \]

the maximum eigenvalue \( \lambda_{\text{max}} \) can extend beyond the theoretical MP bulk edge \( \lambda_{+} \). Specifically, if \( \Delta \) is a large rank-1 perturbation with magnitude \( |\Delta| \), the maximum eigenvalue \( \lambda_{\text{max}} \) that "bleeds out" is given by:

\[ \lambda_{\text{max}} = \sigma^2 \left( \frac{1}{Q} + \frac{|\Delta|^2}{N} \right) \left( 1 + \frac{N}{|\Delta|^2} \right), \]

