In the context of Marchenko-Pastur (MP) theory, studying large low-rank perturbations helps us understand how eigenvalues of a random matrix deviate from the bulk distribution and form spikes. When we model a matrix $\mathbf{W}$ as a sum of a random matrix $\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rand}}$ and a large rank-1 perturbation $\Delta_{\mathrm{large}}$, such that:

\begin{equation}
\mathbf{W} = \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{rand}} + \Delta_{\mathrm{large}},
\end{equation}

the maximum eigenvalue $\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}$ can extend beyond the theoretical MP bulk edge $\lambda_{+}$. Specifically, if $\Delta$ is a large rank-1 perturbation with magnitude $|\Delta|$, the maximum eigenvalue $\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}$ that "bleeds out" is given by:

\begin{equation}
\lambda_{\mathrm{max}} = \sigma^2 \left( \dfrac{1}{Q} + \dfrac{|\Delta|^2}{N} \right) \left( 1 + \dfrac{N}{|\Delta|^2} \right),
\end{equation}

